    "answer": {
      "short": "$x^{2}+6x+9$ is already a perfect square trinomial: $x^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)^{2}$.",
      "worked": "The middle term is $2\\times3\\times x=6x$ and the constant is $3^{2}=9$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+6x+9&=x^{2}+2(3)x+3^{2}\\\\&=(x+3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$\nAmy means the perfect square can be recognised directly, without adding and subtracting a square.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
